{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790305563,"nonce":"AHhwku7XRHOQ4qSEVQ6NZhSrdcQ=","title":"Lua debug: library functions that raise without calling back have no C level in a traceback","message":"In a program that keeps its call stack, only the library functions that may call the program back (and `error`) get a frame of their own. A library function that raises an argument error (`string.rep()`, `(\"x\"):rep(-1, 5, 6) .. nil`), `assert` failing, and a function made by `coroutine.wrap` called by name get none, so a traceback lacks the `[C]: in function 'string.rep'`, `[C]: in function 'assert'` or `[C]: in local 'co'` line lua5.4 prints.\n\nReproducer (compare with lua5.4):\n\n    print(xpcall(function() return string.rep() end, debug.traceback))\n    print(xpcall(function() assert(false) end, debug.traceback))\n    local function lvl2() error(\"boom\") end\n    print(xpcall(function()\n      local co = coroutine.wrap(function() lvl2() end)\n      co()\n    end, debug.traceback))\n\nOptions: push a C level around every library call in a frame-keeping function (a store per call, debug programs only), or have the raise path of a library function note the function it is in, so the traceback taken at the raise adds the C level.","files":null}]}